## 3. VECTOR II: AIRBORNE MIXTURE-OF-EXPERTS ("MoE IN THE SKY"), NON-EXPENDABLE STANDOFF OVERSEERS, & REAR-HEMISPHERE TERMINAL IMMUNITY (`INVARIANT 8 & 17`)

### 3.1 The Mechanistic Mismatch: High-CapEx Sensor Attrition & Forward-Hemisphere Spoofing
Legacy unitary autonomous platforms suffer from two structural flaws:
1. **The Sensor Attrition Tax:** Every unitary platform bolts its most expensive, supply-chain-constrained components—cooled thermal imagers, optical cameras, RF receivers, and neural processing units (NPUs)—directly onto a single attritable airframe. Losing the airframe destroys $80\%$ of the electronics CapEx on every single cycle.
2. **Forward-Hemisphere Terminal Spoofing:** Because a unitary platform must point its optical camera or sensor *forward* during the final 300 meters of approach, its sensors look directly into the highest-intensity zone of the defender’s optical dazzlers, obscurants, and point-source electronic warfare.

### 3.2 The OPSTACK Isomorphism: Physical Mixture-of-Experts (MoE), Standoff Overseer CapEx Recycling & 3-Stage Degraded-Mode State Machine
In machine learning, a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture separates heavy multi-modal routing/perception from lightweight execution. Mapped to an abstract multi-agent swarm of $N$ robotic nodes without creating a decapitation single-point-of-failure (`Invariant 1`), the fleet decouples into **Non-Expendable Standoff Specialist Overseers** (high-CapEx long-range perception) and **Attritable Terminal Effector Nodes with 3-Stage Control Fallback**:

1. **Tiered Sensor Disaggregation & Standoff Survival (Zero High-CapEx Sensor Attrition):**
   * **Standoff Specialist Overseers ($\mathcal{E}_{\text{Opt}}, \mathcal{E}_{\text{Ac}}, \mathcal{E}_{\text{RF}}$ — $20\text{--}30\%$ of fleet):** Each overseer models a single specialized long-range perception modality (Optical, Acoustic, or Passive RF Time-Difference-of-Arrival) plus local inference compute. **Overseers remain outside terminal attrition envelopes**, triangulating target state vectors from standoff orbit or ground stasis and recycling their sensor CapEx across $K \in [10, 50]$ operational cycles.
   * **Attritable Terminal Effector Nodes ($\mathcal{E}_{\text{Kin}}$ — $70\text{--}80\%$ of fleet):** Omit high-cost long-range acquisition arrays and active RF transmitters (`Invariant 18`), carrying only baseline actuation, inertial state estimation, a rear-hemisphere optical/acoustic receiver, and a low-cost short-range terminal perception primitive.

2. **The 3-Stage Degraded-Mode Control State Machine (Zero Decapitation Vulnerability):**
   To eliminate the hub-and-spoke single point of failure where losing standoff overseers disables the effector fleet, each terminal node $\mathcal{E}_{\text{Kin}}$ executes a deterministic 3-stage control-theoretic fallback:
   * **Stage 1 — Rear-Hemisphere Standoff-Guided Transit (Overseer Quorum Intact):** During mid-course transit, the terminal node gates off its forward perception primitive (mitigating forward-hemisphere optical/EW spoofing) and updates its trajectory vector ($\mathbf{B}_{\text{Swarm}}$) via line-of-sight rear-hemisphere optical/ultrasonic signaling from the Standoff Overseers.
   * **Stage 2 — Local Terminal Perception Lock (Overseer Link Severed or Terminal Boundary Reached):** Upon entering the short-range terminal boundary—or immediately if Standoff Overseer line-of-sight is lost—the node enables its local short-range perception primitive to track the target state within the cone previously bounded by the swarm.
   * **Stage 3 — Bounded-CEP Inertial Dead-Reckoning & Mandatory LOAC Art. 57(2)(b) Safe-Abort Fallback (Total Perception Degradation):** If both Standoff Overseer signaling and local terminal perception are simultaneously degraded ($\text{SNR}_{\text{Total}} < \gamma_{\min}$), the node propagates its last validated kinematic state vector $\mathbf{B}_{\text{Swarm}}(t_{\text{last}})$ via standard textbook Proportional Navigation ($\mathbf{a}_{\text{cmd}} = N' V_c \dot{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_{\text{last}}$) **if and only if** the target is a static, pre-validated military objective (Art. 52(2)) and accumulated IMU drift covariance remains strictly inside the objective's physical footprint ($\text{CEP}_{\text{IMU}}(\Delta t_{\text{blind}}) \le R_{\text{MilObj}}$ for $\Delta t_{\text{blind}} \le \tau_{\text{CEP\_Max}}$). Pursuant to **Geneva Protocol I Art. 51(4) & Art. 57(2)(b)**, if blind drift time exceeds $\tau_{\text{CEP\_Max}}$ or the objective is mobile/unconfirmed near civilian objects, the state machine triggers an immediate **Mandatory Safe-Abort / Disarm State Transition** (`commit_kinetic_state = False`).

3. **Environmental Gating & Cross-Modal BFT Quorum Routing (`Invariant 17`):**
   While Overseer quorum is active, each Standoff Overseer class $m \in \{\text{Opt}, \text{Ac}, \text{RF}\}$ computes a local Signal-to-Noise Ratio ($\text{SNR}_m$) and bearing unit vector $\hat{\mathbf{b}}_m$. The collective guidance vector $\mathbf{B}_{\text{Swarm}}$ is gated dynamically by normalized confidence weights $w_m$:
   $$w_m = \frac{\max(0,\; \text{SNR}_m - \gamma_{\min})}{\sum_{k} \max(0,\; \text{SNR}_k - \gamma_{\min})}, \qquad \mathbf{B}_{\text{Swarm}} = \sum_{m \in \{\text{Opt}, \text{Ac}, \text{RF}\}} w_m \hat{\mathbf{b}}_m$$
   When environmental obscurants degrade optical perception ($\text{SNR}_{\text{Opt}} \to 0 \implies w_{\text{Opt}} = 0$), routing weight shifts automatically to the acoustic and passive-RF modalities. Reusing Standoff Overseers across $K$ waves reduces amortized fleet sensor CapEx by $>85\%$ without introducing a centralized decapitation vulnerability.

4. **Lateral Peer-to-Peer Stigmergic Coupling (Couzin/Reynolds Wavefront Gossip & Probabilistic Salvo Redundancy, `Invariant 6 & 8`):**
   Terminal nodes $\mathcal{E}_{\text{Kin}, i}$ avoid star-topology dependence on Standoff Overseers by continuously observing their **$k$-nearest lateral neighbors** ($j \in \mathcal{N}_i$) via short-range optical/ultrasonic kinematics:
   * **Couzin/Reynolds Wavefront Velocity Alignment (Non-Line-of-Sight Relay):** Each node $i$ blends the Standoff Overseer unit bearing vector $\mathbf{B}_{\text{Swarm}}$ (scaled by nominal cruise speed $v_{\text{cruise}}$) with the local velocity alignment of its lateral neighbors (Couzin et al., 2005):
     $$\mathbf{v}_{i}(t + \Delta t) = \alpha v_{\text{cruise}} \mathbf{B}_{\text{Swarm}} + (1 - \alpha) \frac{1}{|\mathcal{N}_i|} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i} \left(\mathbf{v}_j(t) + \tau_{\text{react}}\dot{\mathbf{v}}_j(t)\right)$$
     If terrain masks $80\%$ of the fleet from the Standoff Overseers, a single flank node that receives a state update initiates a heading change that propagates across the non-line-of-sight neighbor graph in milliseconds at **$0.0\text{ W}$ RF emission**.
   * **Probabilistic Salvo Redundancy ($K_k^*$ Multi-Node Assignment) & Attrition Handover:** Because low-cost airframes are expendable whereas airborne inference compute is the scarce bottleneck, 1-to-1 target assignment is suboptimal whenever single-node transit survival $p_k < 1$. Instead, Standoff Overseers pre-compute a **Required Redundancy Quota ($K_k^*$)** for each objective $k$ as an O(1) lookup based on Target Value ($V_k$, dictating required completion confidence $P_{\text{req}}(V_k) \in [0.80, 0.99]$) and Terrain-Masked Corridor Survival Probability ($p_k \in (0, 1)$, where longer tree-line or nap-of-the-earth covered routes carry higher $p_k$ than short open-field vectors):
     $$1 - (1 - p_k)^{K_k^*} \ge P_{\text{req}}(V_k) \implies K_k^* = \left\lceil \frac{\ln\left(1 - P_{\text{req}}(V_k)\right)}{\ln(1 - p_k)} \right\rceil$$
     High-assurance/undefended targets ($p_k \to 0.95$) receive $K_k^* = 1$ node, whereas heavily defended, high-value emitters ($p_k = 0.40, P_{\text{req}} = 0.95$) automatically receive $K_k^* = 6$ redundant terminal nodes along the highest-cover terrain corridor. Trailing nodes monitor lead-node completion: once an objective is confirmed neutralized early via OOB/optical bloom, surviving excess nodes in that salvo dynamically spill over to secondary objective $\mathbf{x}_B = \mathbf{x}_A + \Delta \mathbf{x}_{\text{salvo}}$.

